// The `cgroup-delegate` loophole, shipped by the OFFICIAL `cgroup-delegate` pack —
// the jail's own control over its own cgroup, which is what `yolo-cglimit` uses.
//
// THIS MANIFEST DECLARES NO DAEMON, AND THAT IS NOT AN OMISSION.  The delegate is
// yolo's own IN-PROCESS goroutine on the launcher side (startCgroupDelegateInProc),
// bound to an AF_UNIX socket, and it is the LAST service in the tree that is not on
// the loopback-TLS transport.  The reason is SO_PEERCRED: `create_and_join` writes the
// peer's HOST-NAMESPACE pid — read off the connection by the kernel, never sent by the
// caller — into the job cgroup's cgroup.procs, and that write is what moves the caller
// into the cgroup.  A TCP connection carries no peer credential at all, and a
// loopback-TLS FRONT would be worse than nothing, because SO_PEERCRED on the upstream
// socket would then attest YOLO'S OWN pid (see startCgroupDelegate in
// internal/cli/run/loopholesruntime.go, and docs/design/security-shim.md §2).
//
// So what this file IS, precisely: a SWITCH and an identity.  It is the thing that
// makes the delegate opt-in like everything else, and the thing that puts it in `yolo
// loopholes list`, `yolo check` and the briefing under the same vocabulary as every
// other loophole.  It declares no daemon, no bind mount, no device and no jail_env,
// because yolo's own run pipeline still does all of that — what it no longer does is
// decide, by itself, that the delegate should be running.
//
// IT WAS PRESENCE-ACTIVATED UNTIL 2026-08-18, and that is the whole reason it is here.
// The delegate started whenever the platform allowed — "Linux only, cgroup v2 only" —
// with NO CONFIG KEY ANYWHERE.  That is precisely the shape R1 deletes, in a host-side
// service, and it was the last one left.  OQ-A4 ruled it opt-in with no exception:
// "the moment one builtin stays presence-activated, 'presence never activates' stops
// being a rule anyone can rely on while reading the code."
{
  "name": "cgroup-delegate",
  "description": "Lets a jail set its own cgroup limits (yolo-cglimit). The delegate hands the jail control of ITS OWN cgroup; it reads no host state.",
  "version": 1,

  // OFF, and this one has a STATED, ACCEPTED COST rather than a migration: yolo-cglimit
  // stops working out of the box (docs/design/loophole-activation.md OQ-A4, said in the
  // ruling itself rather than discovered afterwards).  Nothing in the jail restores it
  // except asking:
  //
  //   "packs": ["cgroup-delegate"],
  //   "loopholes": { "cgroup-delegate": { "enabled": true } }
  //
  // R4's subject is HOST ACCESS, and the delegate is the weakest host crossing yolo
  // has — it delegates the jail's OWN cgroup rather than reading anything about the
  // machine.  OQ-A4 says so and rules it opt-in anyway, because R1 is about the
  // MECHANISM rather than the severity.  A rule with one exception is two rules.
  "default_enabled": false,

  // Linux-only, DECLARED rather than probed, exactly as `audio` and `journal` declare
  // it: cgroups are a Linux kernel feature, so on darwin there is nothing to install
  // and `platforms` is the field that says so without sending a macOS reader after a
  // package.
  //
  // THE cgroup-v2 CHECK IS NOT HERE and must not be moved here.  "Linux" is a property
  // of the machine class; "this kernel is booted with cgroup v2 and the controllers are
  // delegated" is a runtime fact about one host, and it stays where it is —
  // startCgroupDelegateInProc, which is also the only place that can report it in the
  // terms an operator can act on.  `requires.file_exists` could probe a path, but its
  // ANSWER leaks through `yolo loopholes list` as an inactive reason and it would still
  // not cover the delegation question.
  "platforms": [
    "linux"
  ],

  // `none` means NO DAEMON, not a different transport — see the header for why this
  // loophole's socket is yolo's own in-process listener and why it stays AF_UNIX.
  // Declaring `loopback-tls` here would be a claim about a publication that never
  // happens: nothing writes cgroup-delegate.endpoint, and the jail reads
  // YOLO_SERVICE_CGROUP_DELEGATE_SOCKET, which is the _SOCKET spelling precisely
  // because the value IS a socket.
  "transport": "none",
  "lifecycle": "external"

  // No `settings`.  What the delegate may do is not configurable — it is one operation
  // (create a job cgroup and move the caller into it) against the jail's own subtree,
  // and the limits themselves are arguments the jail passes per call.
  //
  // No `doctor_cmd` either.  The health question is "is the socket there", which the
  // entrypoint already answers in the jail's own boot output ("cgroup delegate:
  // available / not available"), and a host-side check that only ever printed the
  // platform back would make `yolo check` longer without making it more informative.
}
